/- ========================================================================= -/
/- Simple formulation of rings (commutative, with 1) as type "(A)ring".      -/
/- ========================================================================= -/
/- File: HolLibrary/RingTheory.lean

  Lean4 translation by Chris Walton 2026
  Original (c) Copyright, John Harrison 2019-2025
           (c) Copyright, Daniel J. Bernstein 2024-2025

  A translation of HOL Light's `Library/ringtheory.ml`, by hand, in file
  order, in the style of HolLibrary/Card.lean and HolLibrary/BitMatch.lean
  (see there and HolMath/Base.lean for the conventions).  ringtheory.ml
  begins with `needs "Library/binomial.ml"`, `needs
  "Library/pocklington.ml"` and `needs "Library/card.ml"`; the theorems it
  uses from them (and from their parents iter.ml, prime.ml and wo.ml) come
  through CoreThms1-8 with the core ones, and their conversions through
  `RingTheoryDeps`.  Each group of sections of ringtheory.ml is a bundle (a
  `structure`) built by a `setup` function; SessionLibrary/RingTheory.lean
  builds the bundles in order and exports every name under its HOL Light
  name.

  EVALUATION ORDER.  OCaml evaluates function arguments, tuples and list
  literals right to left, so in a proof the rules nearer the end of the text
  are evaluated first.  Where one proof applies two or more rules that print
  or use counters (MESON, ARITH_RULE, NUMBER_RULE, INTEGER_RULE, INT_ARITH,
  SET_RULE, TAUT, ...) they are bound first, last one first.  `let ... and
  ...` is left to right.
-/

